// Greenhouse humidity (2026-09-26, gardening depth: the air the crops grow in).
// Read by src/systems/farming/humidity.rs, which carries the model and the
// reasoning; this file carries the numbers and where each one came from.
// Every source below was read on 2026-09-26.
//
// WHAT THE GAME TEACHES. A greenhouse's hardest daily skill is humidity. The
// crops breathe out nearly all the water they are given, the closed air grows
// humid, and gray mold and the mildews follow (data/garden/pests.ron) unless
// the grower ventilates. The balance is shown on the Garden panel for each
// grow room, and on each crop's card against its plants.csv window. The
// mushroom room teaches the other side: fruiting mushrooms need air far wetter
// than a home's, and holding a room there costs water and power every day.
//
// THE MODEL, in one paragraph (humidity.rs has the detail). Each indoor grow
// room (the rooms the grow machines stand in) holds its water vapour, grams
// per cubic metre of air. Every tick it GAINS the water its growing,
// watered crops breathe out (plants.csv water_liters_per_day x the plants in
// each unit x vapour_share, over the room's volume) and LOSES vapour to air
// exchange with the home's air: air changes an hour (the room's own leakage,
// base_air_changes_per_hour, plus any powered exhaust fans) times the
// difference from the home air's vapour. It is solved exactly per tick, so a
// frame and a clock jump agree, and it cannot pass saturation (the rest
// condenses on the leaves and the glazing). Relative humidity is the vapour
// over what saturated air at the room's temperature holds, by the FAO-56
// formula below. Outdoor fields use the weather's humidity; a crop in no known
// room uses the home air's.
//
// TIME. The air runs on the game clock, the one the day and night run on
// (an hour of it is 50 seconds of play), not the crops' growth speed: the
// crops' litres a day and the air changes an hour are both read on it, so
// the humidity a room settles at is the physical one whatever the growth
// speed, and a heat-and-vent wears off over game hours, the way it does over
// an evening. The irrigation bills the home's tanks per real day instead
// (farming::mod, irrigation_demand_lpm): the water supply's clock, a
// separate question.
//
// SOURCES (cited by short name in the entries):
//   FAO-56     Allen, R.G., Pereira, L.S., Raes, D. and Smith, M., "Crop
//              evapotranspiration: Guidelines for computing crop water
//              requirements", FAO Irrigation and Drainage Paper 56, FAO, Rome,
//              1998. Chapter 1, https://www.fao.org/4/x0490e/x0490e04.htm ;
//              Chapter 3, https://www.fao.org/4/x0490e/x0490e07.htm ; Annex 3,
//              https://www.fao.org/4/x0490e/x0490e0k.htm
//   UGA B792   Ferrarezi, R.S. and Worley, J.W., "Greenhouses: Heating,
//              Ventilation, and Cooling", University of Georgia Extension
//              Bulletin 792, published 4 November 2025,
//              https://fieldreport.caes.uga.edu/publications/B792/greenhouses-heating-ventilation-and-cooling/
//   UMass      Wick, R.L., revised by Madeiras, A., "Botrytis Blight of
//              Greenhouse Crops", UMass Amherst Center for Agriculture, Food,
//              and the Environment fact sheet, last updated 5 June 2019,
//              https://www.umass.edu/agriculture-food-environment/greenhouse-floriculture/fact-sheets/botrytis-blight-of-greenhouse-crops
//   AC Infinity  "CLOUDLINE T12 Inline Fan 12 inch", AC Infinity product
//              specifications (read 2026-09-26; the page carries no date),
//              https://acinfinity.com/cloudline-t12-quiet-inline-fan-12-with-temperature-humidity-vpd-controller/
//   Affinity   "Affinity laws", Wikipedia, last edited 20 November 2025,
//              https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Affinity_laws
//   Bakker 1991  Bakker, J.C., "Analysis of humidity effects on growth and
//              production of glasshouse fruit vegetables", PhD thesis,
//              Landbouwuniversiteit Wageningen, 1991, abstract at
//              https://research.wur.nl/en/publications/analysis-of-humidity-effects-on-growth-and-production-of-glasshou/
//   AC Infinity T7  "CLOUDFORGE T7 Plant Humidifier, 15L", AC Infinity
//              product specifications (read 2026-09-26; the page carries no
//              date),
//              https://acinfinity.com/cloudforge-t7-plant-humidifier-15l-with-vpd-humidity-controls/ ;
//              also sold by mushroom-growing suppliers, e.g.
//              https://www.redwoodmushroomsupply.com/products/ac-infinity-cloudforge-t7-humidifier
//   UF/IFAS SS662  Hameed, K., Cotter, V.T., Liao, H.-L. and Hsu, C.-M.,
//              "D.I.Y. FunGuide: Grow Your Own Oyster Mushrooms at Home",
//              UF/IFAS Extension publication SL448/SS662 (no date on the page
//              read), https://ask.ifas.ufl.edu/publication/SS662
//   Agrodok 40  Oei, P., with contributions by van Nieuwenhuijzen, B.,
//              "Small-scale mushroom cultivation: oyster, shiitake and wood ear
//              mushrooms", Agrodok 40, Agromisa Foundation and CTA,
//              Wageningen, first edition 2005,
//              https://www.agromisa.org/wp-content/uploads/Agrodok-40-Small-scale-mushroom-cultivation.pdf
//   Kim 2013   Kim, S.Y., Kim, M.K., Im, C.H., Kim, K.H., Kim, D.S., Kim,
//              T.S., Park, K.K., Lee, S.D. and Ryu, J.S., "Optimal relative
//              humidity for Pleurotus eryngii cultivation", Journal of
//              Mushroom 11(3): 131-136, 2013, doi:10.14480/JM.2013.11.3.131,
//              https://koreascience.or.kr/article/JAKO201307954512342.page
(
    // -- RELATIVE AND ABSOLUTE HUMIDITY: the psychrometric formula ---------------
    //
    // Saturation vapour pressure, FAO-56 Chapter 3, equation 11:
    // "e°(T) = 0.6108 exp[17.27(T)/(T + 237.3)]", with "e°(T) saturation vapour
    // pressure at the air temperature T [kPa]" and "T air temperature [°C]".
    // Its worked Example 3 gives "e°(Tmax) = 3.075 kPa" at 24.5 °C; a test
    // checks the formula against it.
    // Relative humidity, FAO-56 Chapter 3 (equation 10): "The relative
    // humidity (RH) expresses the degree of saturation of the air as a ratio of
    // the actual (ea) to the saturation (e°(T)) vapour pressure at the same
    // temperature (T)".
    // Vapour in grams per m3 from its pressure is the ideal gas law with water
    // vapour's gas constant, which is dry air's over the molecular-weight
    // ratio. FAO-56 Annex 3: "R specific gas constant = 287 [J kg-1 K-1]" and
    // "ε ratio molecular weight of water vapour/dry air = 0.622", so water
    // vapour's is 287 / 0.622 = 461.4 J/(kg K). At the room's 21 °C saturated
    // air holds 18.3 g/m3.
    svp_a_kpa: 0.6108,
    svp_b: 17.27,
    svp_c_c: 237.3,
    dry_air_gas_constant: 287.0,
    vapour_weight_ratio: 0.622,

    // The grow rooms' temperature, °C. A GAME CHOICE, the same 21 °C the pests
    // take for the climate-controlled rooms (data/garden/pests.ron
    // indoor_temp_c; a test keeps the two equal): the HVAC sim runs per room
    // and does not reach the grow rooms yet.
    room_temp_c: 21.0,

    // -- WHERE THE WATER GOES ----------------------------------------------------
    //
    // vapour_share 1.0: the share of a crop's plants.csv water that goes into
    // the room's air. plants.csv water_liters_per_day IS the FAO crop
    // evapotranspiration (Kc x ET0 x the plant's area; see its header and
    // data/garden/yields.ron, WATER), and evapotranspiration is by definition
    // water that leaves as vapour. FAO-56 Chapter 1: "The combination of two
    // separate processes whereby water is lost on the one hand from the soil
    // surface by evaporation and on the other hand from the crop by
    // transpiration is referred to as evapotranspiration (ET)", and "Nearly
    // all water taken up is lost by transpiration and only a tiny fraction is
    // used within the plant." The tiny fraction kept is on top of ET, not part
    // of it, so all of the listed water becomes vapour. Only crops that are
    // growing and watered count: a thirsty crop closes its stomata, a ripe one
    // is no longer drawing water in the game (the same crops the irrigation
    // bills).
    vapour_share: 1.0,

    // -- HOW THE AIR IS EXCHANGED -------------------------------------------------
    //
    // base_air_changes_per_hour 0.5: the room's own leakage with every fan off.
    // UGA B792, "Table 2. Natural Air Exchanges for Greenhouses": "New
    // Construction, double layer plastic film | 0.5 to 1.0" air exchanges per
    // hour ("New Construction, glass or fiberglass | 0.75 to 1"), footnoted
    // "Low wind or protection from wind reduces the air exchange rate." The
    // home's grow rooms are indoors, out of the wind altogether, so the low
    // end of the tightest construction is taken.
    //
    // With that alone the home's 3-person greenhouse does not stay healthy: at
    // its full planting (about 540 L a day breathed out into 2,970 m3) the
    // balance settles above saturation, so it runs at 100% with water
    // condensing. The home therefore carries one exhaust fan there (the
    // "exhaust_fan" machine in data/machines/home.ron). The one-person home's
    // greenhouse (about 200 L a day) settles near 68% on its leakage alone and
    // carries none.
    base_air_changes_per_hour: 0.5,

    // THE EXHAUST FAN'S CONTROLLER. The fan in the machine catalog is a 12 inch
    // EC inline fan with a humidity controller, AC Infinity CLOUDLINE T12:
    // "Total Airflow: 1604 CFM" (2,725 m3/h), "Max Power Draw: 250 W", and a
    // controller with "temperature, humidity, and VPD triggers". Its motor is
    // speed controlled, so it runs as fast as the room needs to hold the
    // setpoint and no faster: flat out while the room is above it, at the
    // speed that holds the setpoint once there, idle when the room is drier.
    //
    // fan_setpoint_rh 0.80: a GAME CHOICE five points under the line UMass
    // gives for gray mold, "relative humidity 85% or greater".
    //
    // fan_power_exponent 3: the power a fan draws goes as the cube of its
    // speed. Affinity: the affinity laws "(also known as the "Fan Laws" or
    // "Pump Laws") for pumps/fans are used in hydraulics, hydronics and/or HVAC
    // to express the relationship between variables involved in pump or fan
    // performance"; their Law 1 has flow proportional to shaft speed and Law 3
    // power proportional to its cube. So the T12 holding the full greenhouse at
    // 80% (about half speed) draws about 30 W, not 250.
    fan_setpoint_rh: 0.80,
    fan_power_exponent: 3.0,

    // THE HUMIDIFIER (the mushroom room, 2026-09-26). The mushroom racks'
    // crops are fungi, whose plants.csv window is far above what their room
    // holds on its own: oyster 85 to 95%, while the home's six racks breathe
    // out only about 7 L a day into the 300 m3 mushroom room, which settles
    // near 48% on its leakage (the one-person home's two racks, about 41%).
    // So each home carries a humidifier there (the "humidifier" machine in
    // data/machines/home.ron and home_solo.ron).
    //
    // The machine is an ultrasonic grow-room humidifier with an onboard
    // humidity controller, AC Infinity CLOUDFORGE T7: "Output Rate: 43.95 fl
    // oz/h (1300 ml/h)", "Power Consumption: 100 W", "Water Capacity: 3.96 gal
    // (15 l)", "10 humidity levels", and "Backlit display adjusts VPD and
    // humidity triggers, timers, and output levels, while AI programming uses
    // PID control to accelerate or slow output for a precise target climate."
    // Its water: "Automatic water valve with tubing refills from an external
    // water source", so it is plumbed to the garden's irrigation line and
    // draws from the home's tanks with the crops' water: it runs only while
    // the irrigation runs and the tanks are not dry (the crops' own gate).
    // Its own 15 L reservoir (about 11 hours at full output) is not modelled:
    // it stops with the tanks. Every litre it puts out is taken to become
    // vapour in the room (the mist evaporates; its latent heat comes from the
    // room air, which the rooms' fixed 21 °C does not track). The litres are
    // billed on the irrigation's clock (see TIME above).
    //
    // Its controller holds the room at humidifier_setpoint_rh: flat out below
    // it, the output that holds it once there, off above it (the PID the
    // maker describes, reduced to what it settles on). Its DRAW is its 100 W
    // times its output share: a GAME CHOICE, since the maker publishes only
    // the full-output draw. The basis: an ultrasonic vaporizer turns water to
    // mist at a steady energy per litre, and a lower level runs it less of the
    // time, so the draw falls with the output (about 77 W per L/h here).
    //
    // What it costs, at the rooms' leakage and the home air's 37% (at 21 °C):
    // holding 300 m3 at 90% takes 0.5 x (16.5 - 6.9) g/m3 x 300 m3 = about
    // 1.45 L an hour carried away, less what the racks breathe out. The
    // family home's six racks: about 1.14 L/h, 88% output, about 88 W and
    // 2.1 kWh and 27 L of water a day. The one-person home's two racks: the
    // T7 runs flat out and the room settles near 88%, inside oyster's window
    // but short of the setpoint, at 100 W, 2.4 kWh and 31 L a day. A whole
    // room is a costly thing to humidify for 100 kcal of mushrooms a day;
    // growers of this size fruit in a tent around the racks instead, which
    // the game does not model yet (the Progress note in
    // docs/design/gameplay-gaps-2026-09-25.md).
    //
    // humidifier_setpoint_rh 0.90: UF/IFAS SS662's fruiting chamber, "90%
    // humidity controlled by humidifiers or foggers", where "The pinheads
    // expand into the first flush of oyster mushrooms if proper air exchange
    // and humidity are maintained". Agrodok 40 agrees in range: "ensure that
    // high humidity is maintained: 90 to 95%", then "When the pinheads have
    // grown to a size of 1 cm, the humidity should be lowered somewhat to 85%
    // by passing fresh air through the room."
    //
    // humidified_fan_margin_rh 0.05: in a room a humidifier holds, the exhaust
    // fans' controller works this far above the humidifier's setpoint instead
    // of at fan_setpoint_rh (0.95 in the mushroom room), so the fan never
    // dries what the humidifier wets. A GAME CHOICE: the dead band a paired
    // humidify and exhaust controller is set with. And no damp-disease notice
    // is given there: the damp is meant.
    humidifier_setpoint_rh: 0.90,
    humidified_fan_margin_rh: 0.05,

    // THE VENTILATE CONTROL (pests.ron `ventilate`) replaces the room's air
    // once, the heat-and-vent UMass describes: "To prevent excessive humidity
    // and dew formation, turn on the heat and open the vents. The warm humid
    // air is ventilated to the outside as the cooler outside air is drawn into
    // the greenhouse." It is one air change of well-mixed air, which removes
    // 1 - 1/e (63%) of the vapour above the home air's; the crops breathe it
    // back within an hour or two, which is why UMass adds that it may need
    // repeating "several times during evening hours" and "can be automated",
    // and why the fan exists.
    //
    // notice_above_rh 0.85: the player is told once when a grow room reaches
    // it, UMass's line for gray mold: "Germination of spores and infection of
    // the host is dependent on a film of moisture for 8 to 12 hours, relative
    // humidity 85% or greater, and temperatures 55 - 75°F."
    notice_above_rh: 0.85,

    // -- WHAT HUMIDITY DOES TO A CROP OUTSIDE ITS WINDOW --------------------------
    //
    // A crop whose air is outside its plants.csv humidity_min..humidity_max has
    // its health capped at 100 x (1 - stress_loss_max x min(1, d /
    // stress_full_at_rh)), where d is how far outside the window it is, and
    // never below health_floor. Gentle on purpose: humidity's own effect on
    // growth is small, and most of the harm humid air does comes through the
    // diseases it favours, which pests.ron carries.
    //
    // stress_loss_max 0.10: Bakker 1991, on glasshouse cucumber, tomato, sweet
    // pepper and eggplant: "the effect of humidity on leaf photosynthesis under
    // normal growing conditions in moderate climates is limited to about 10%",
    // with "yield of tomato was lower at continuously high humidity", "Yield of
    // sweet pepper was unaffected" and "Final yield of cucumber was higher at
    // high humidity by day".
    // stress_full_at_rh 0.25: that ~10% is over the range Bakker studied,
    // "0.3 to 0.9 kPa Vapour Pressure Deficit", which at the rooms' 21 °C is
    // relative humidity from 88% down to 64%: about 25 points. A GAME CHOICE
    // to read it as the whole loss 25 points outside the window.
    // health_floor 20: the house floor, the same as a nutrient shortage, a
    // pest and soil pH (soil::NUTRIENT_HEALTH_FLOOR); a green crop's cap never
    // gets near it, a fungus in dry air can reach it (FUNGI below).
    stress_loss_max: 0.10,
    stress_full_at_rh: 0.25,

    // FUNGI (plants.csv needs_light false: the oyster, shiitake and button
    // mushrooms) BELOW their window are not a green crop: a mushroom has no
    // leaf to close, and a pin in dry air dries out. Agrodok 40: "Good control
    // of the humidity during cropping is very important for all types of
    // mushroom", and on shiitake, "If the humidity is also relatively low (60
    // to 70%), then cracks may appear in the caps". So below its window a
    // fungus is capped at 100 x (1 - (d / fungi_loss_full_at_rh)^2) for d
    // points under the window's low edge (never below health_floor); above it
    // (wet air) the green crops' cap above still applies.
    //
    // fungi_loss_full_at_rh 0.47, from Kim 2013, the one measured series of
    // yield against fixed humidity found, on the king oyster (P. eryngii, the
    // oyster's genus). Its Table 3 totals the harvest at "15,670" g at 90%
    // humidity, "14,985" at 80% and "12,817" at 70%: 4.4% lost 10 points under
    // the best and 18.2% lost 20 under, the loss growing as the square of the
    // shortfall. (d / 0.47)^2 gives 4.5% and 18.1% there. The abstract adds
    // that "as humidity was lower, period of harvest was longer as well as
    // days for pinheading", and its Table 1 has only 77.8% of the thinned
    // bottles reaching harvest at 70%, against 100.0% at 90%. Two readings
    // are GAME CHOICES: it is applied to every fungus (the genus is the
    // oyster's; the shiitake and button mushroom have no such series here),
    // and it is carried past Kim's driest 70% on the same curve, reaching the
    // whole crop 47 points under. It is measured from the window's low edge,
    // oyster's 85%, five points under Kim's best, so it errs kind. An oyster
    // crop in the family home's mushroom room without its humidifier (48%,
    // 37 points under) is held to about 38, where a green crop that far out
    // of its window keeps 90.
    fungi_loss_full_at_rh: 0.47,
    health_floor: 20.0,
)
